"Accelerating Innovation Through Analogy Mining | IJCAI (https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2018/736)\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2018/736\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 30\nL0: #\nL1: \nL2: # Accelerating Innovation Through Analogy Mining\nL3: \nL4: # Accelerating Innovation Through Analogy Mining\nL5: ## Tom Hope, Joel Chan, Aniket Kittur, Dafna Shahaf\nL6: \nL7: Proceedings of the Twenty-Seventh International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence\nL8: \nL9: Best Sister Conferences. Pages 5274-5278. cite0†https://doi.org/10.24963/ijcai.2018/736†doi.org L10: \nL11: cite1†PDF cite2†BibTeX L12: \nL13: * * *\nL14: The availability of large idea repositories (e.g., patents) could significantly accelerate innovation and discovery by providing people inspiration from solutions to analogous problems. However, finding useful analogies in these large, messy, real-world repositories remains a persistent challenge for both humans and computers.\nL15: Previous approaches include costly hand-created databases that do not scale, or machine-learning similarity metrics that struggle to account for structural similarity, which is central to analogy. In this paper we explore the viability and value of learning simple structural representations. Our approach combines crowdsourcing and recurrent neural networks to extract purpose and mechanism vector representations from product descriptions.\nL16: We demonstrate that these learned vectors allow us to find analogies with higher precision and recall than traditional methods. In an ideation experiment, analogies retrieved by our models significantly increased people's likelihood of generating creative ideas.\nL17: Keywords:\nL18: \nL19: Machine Learning: Data Mining\nL20: \nL21: Humans and AI: Human-Computer Interaction\nL22: \nL23: Natural Language Processing: Information Retrieval\nL24: \nL25: Multidisciplinary Topics and Applications: Information Retrieval\nL26: \nL27: Natural Language Processing: NLP Applications and Tools\nL28: \nL29: Copyright © 2026,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelation-Aware Neighborhood Matching Model for Entity Alignment | Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/16606)\nciteturn19view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/16606\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 72\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to main navigation menu cite2†Skip to site footer L1: \nL2: Open Menu\nL3: \nL4: cite3†Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence L5: \nL6:   * cite4†Current L7:   * cite5†Archives L8:   * cite6†About L9:     * cite6†About the Journal L10:     * cite7†Submissions L11:     * cite8†Privacy Statement L12:     * cite9†Contact L13: \nL14: cite10†Search L15: \nL16:   * cite11†Login L17: \nL18: # Relation-Aware Neighborhood Matching Model for Entity Alignment\nL19: ## Authors\nL20: \nL21:   * Yao Zhu Center for Data Science, Peking University\nL22:   * Hongzhi Liu School of Software and Microelectronics, Peking University\nL23:   * Zhonghai Wu National Engineering Center of Software Engineering, Peking University Key Lab of High Confidence Software Technologies (MOE), Peking University\nL24:   * Yingpeng Du School of Software and Microelectronics, Peking University\nL25: \nL26: ## DOI:\nL27: \nL28: cite12†https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v35i5.16606†doi.org L29: \nL30: ## Keywords:\nL31: \nL32: Linked Open Data, Knowledge Graphs & KB Completio\nL33: ## Abstract\nL34: Entity alignment which aims at linking entities with the same meaning from different knowledge graphs (KGs) is a vital step for knowledge fusion. Existing research focused on learning embeddings of entities by utilizing structural information of KGs for entity alignment. These methods can aggregate information from neighboring nodes but may also bring noise from neighbors. Most recently, several researchers attempted to compare neighboring nodes in pairs to enhance the entity alignment.\nL35: However, they ignored the relations between entities which are also important for neighborhood matching. In addition, existing methods paid less attention to the positive interactions between the entity alignment and the relation alignment. To deal with these issues, we propose a novel Relation-aware Neighborhood Matching model named RNM for entity alignment. Specifically, we propose to utilize the neighborhood matching to enhance the entity alignment.\nL36: Besides comparing neighbor nodes when matching neighborhood, we also try to explore useful information from the connected relations. Moreover, an iterative framework is designed to leverage the positive interactions between the entity alignment and the relation alignment in a semi-supervised manner. Experimental results on three real-world datasets demonstrate that the proposed model RNM performs better than state-of-the-art methods.\nL37: ## Downloads\nL38: \nL39:   * cite13†PDF L40: \nL41: ## Published\nL42: \nL43: 2021-05-18\nL44: ## How to Cite\nL45: \nL46: Zhu, Y., Liu, H., Wu, Z., & Du, Y. (2021). Relation-Aware Neighborhood Matching Model for Entity Alignment. Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, 35(5), 4749–4756. https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v35i5.16606\nL47: \nL48: [Button: More Citation Formats ]\nL49: \nL50:   * cite14†ACM L51:   * cite15†ACS L52:   * cite16†APA L53:   * cite17†ABNT L54:   * cite18†Chicago L55:   * cite19†Harvard L56:   * cite20†IEEE L57:   * cite21†MLA L58:   * cite22†Turabian L59:   * cite23†Vancouver L60: \nL61: Download Citation\nL62: \nL63:   * cite24†Endnote/Zotero/Mendeley (RIS) L64:   * cite25†BibTeX L65: ## Issue\nL66: \nL67: cite26†Vol. 35 No. 5: AAAI-21 Technical Tracks 5 L68: \nL69: ## Section\nL70: \nL71: AAAI Technical Track on Data Mining and Knowledge Management\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn19view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://vldb.org/pvldb/vol13/p2326-sun.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://vldb.org/pvldb/vol13/p2326-sun.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.548.pdf)\nciteturn19view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.548.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1259\nL0@P0: Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 9820–9836\nL1@P0: November 12-16, 2024 ©2024 Association for Computational Linguistics\nL2@P0: Extract, Define, Canonicalize: An LLM-based Framework for Knowledge\nL3@P0: Graph Construction\nL4@P0: Bowen Zhang1 and Harold Soh1,2\nL5@P0: 1Dept. of Computer Science, National University of Singapore, 2NUS Smart Systems Institute\nL6@P0: {bowenzhang, harold}@comp.nus.edu.sg\nL7@P0: Abstract\nL8@P0: In this work, we are interested in automated\nL9@P0: methods for knowledge graph creation (KGC)\nL10@P0: from input text. Progress on large language\nL11@P0: models (LLMs) has prompted a series of re\u0002cent works applying them to KGC, e.g., via\nL12@P0: zero/few-shot prompting. Despite successes on\nL13@P0: small domain-specific datasets, these models\nL14@P0: face difficulties scaling up to text common in\nL15@P0: many real-world applications. A principal is\u0002sue is that, in prior methods, the KG schema\nL16@P0: has to be included in the LLM prompt to gen\u0002erate valid triplets; larger and more complex\nL17@P0: schemas easily exceed the LLMs’ context win\u0002dow length. Furthermore, there are scenar\u0002ios where a fixed pre-defined schema is not\nL18@P0: available and we would like the method to con\u0002struct a high-quality KG with a succinct self\u0002generated schema. To address these problems,\nL19@P0: we propose a three-phase framework named\nL20@P0: Extract-Define-Canonicalize (EDC): open in\u0002formation extraction followed by schema def\u0002inition and post-hoc canonicalization. EDC\nL21@P0: is flexible in that it can be applied to settings\nL22@P0: where a pre-defined target schema is available\nL23@P0: and when it is not; in the latter case, it con\u0002structs a schema automatically and applies self\u0002canonicalization. To further improve perfor\u0002mance, we introduce a trained component that\nL24@P0: retrieves schema elements relevant to the input\nL25@P0: text; this improves the LLMs’ extraction per\u0002formance in a retrieval-augmented generation\u0002like manner. We demonstrate on three KGC\nL26@P0: benchmarks that EDC is able to extract high\u0002quality triplets without any parameter tuning\nL27@P0: and with significantly larger schemas compared\nL28@P0: to prior works. Code for EDC is available at\nL29@P0: https://github.com/clear-nus/edc.\nL30@P0: 1 Introduction\nL31@P0: Knowledge graphs (KGs) (Ji et al., 2021) are a\nL32@P0: structured representation of knowledge that orga\u0002nizes interconnected information through graph\nL33@P0: structures, where entities and relations are rep\u0002resented as nodes and edges. They are broadly\nL34@P0: Extract relational triplets from the text: ‘Alan Shepard\nL35@P0: participated in the Apollo 14 mission’\nL36@P0: EDC: Extract-Define-Canonicalize\nL37@P0: Phase 1: Open Information Extraction\nL38@P0: Phase 2: Schema Definition\nL39@P0: Phase 3: Schema Canonicalization\nL40@P0: [Alan Shepard, participatedIn, Apollo 14]\nL41@P0: Write a definition for the relation ‘participatedIn’ in the\nL42@P0: current context\nL43@P0: The subject entity took part in the mission specified\nL44@P0: by the object entity\nL45@P0: The most semantically similar relation in the schema\nL46@P0: is ‘mission’\nL47@P0: Can the relation ‘participatedIn’ be replaced by\nL48@P0: ‘mission’ given the current context?\nL49@P0: Yes! The converted relational triplet is\nL50@P0: [Alan Shepard, mission, Apollo 14]\nL51@P0: Figure 1: A high-level illustration of Extract-Define\u0002Canonicalize (EDC) for Knowledge Graph Construc\u0002tion.\nL52@P0: used in a variety of downstream tasks such as\nL53@P0: decision-making (Guo et al., 2021; Lan et al.,\nL54@P0: 2020), question-answering (Huang et al., 2019; Ya\u0002sunaga et al., 2021), and recommendation (Guo\nL55@P0: et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2019). However, knowl\u0002edge graph construction (KGC) is inherently chal\u0002lenging: the task requires competence in under\u0002standing syntax and semantics to generate a con\u0002sistent, concise, and meaningful knowledge graph.\nL56@P0: As such, KGC predominantly relies on intensive\nL57@P0: human labor (Ye et al., 2022). KGC is a broad\nL58@P0: problem and in this work, we focus on the task\nL59@P0: of relational triplet extraction as it is crucial for\nL60@P0-1: 9820KGC. Following previous works (Ye et al., 2022;\nL61@P1: Melnyk et al., 2022; Bi et al., 2024), we still refer\nL62@P1: to the task we are addressing as KGC.\nL63@P1: Recent attempts to automate KGC (Zhong et al.,\nL64@P1: 2023; Ye et al., 2022) have employed large lan\u0002guage models (LLMs) in view of their remark\u0002able natural language understanding and generation\nL65@P1: capabilities. LLM-based KGC methods employ\nL66@P1: various innovative prompt-based techniques, such\nL67@P1: as multi-turn conversation (Wei et al., 2023) and\nL68@P1: code generation (Bi et al., 2024), to generate entity\u0002relation triplets that represent the knowledge graph.\nL69@P1: However, these methods are currently limited to\nL70@P1: small and domain-specific scenarios — to ensure\nL71@P1: the validity of generated triplets, schema informa\u0002tion (e.g., possible entity and relation types) has to\nL72@P1: be included in the prompt. Complex datasets (e.g.,\nL73@P1: Wikipedia) typically require large schemas that\nL74@P1: exceed the context window length or can be ig\u0002nored by the LLMs (Wadhwa et al., 2023). Further\u0002more, pre-defined schemas are not always avail\u0002able — the users might not have pre-determined\nL75@P1: or fixed intentions about what information is of\nL76@P1: interest in advance but still would like to extract\nL77@P1: intrinsically high-quality KGs. It is unclear how\nL78@P1: existing methods will work in such situations.\nL79@P1: To address these problems, we propose Ex\u0002tract-Define-Canonicalize (EDC), a structured\nL80@P1: approach for KGC: the key idea is to decompose\nL81@P1: KGC into three primary phases corresponding to\nL82@P1: three subtasks (Fig. 1):\nL83@P1: 1. Open Information Extraction: extract a list\nL84@P1: of entity-relation triplets from the input text\nL85@P1: freely.\nL86@P1: 2. Schema Definition: generate a definition for\nL87@P1: each component of the schema, e.g. entity\nL88@P1: type and relation type, induced by triplets ob\u0002tained in the extraction phase.\nL89@P1: 3. Schema Canonicalization: use the schema\nL90@P1: definitions to standardize the triplets such\nL91@P1: that semantically-equivalent entities/relations\nL92@P1: types have the same noun/relation phrase.\nL93@P1: Each phase exploits the strengths of LLMs:\nL94@P1: the Extract subtask leverages recent findings that\nL95@P1: LLMs are effective open information extractors (Li\nL96@P1: et al., 2023; Han et al., 2023) — they can extract\nL97@P1: semantically correct and meaningful triplets. How\u0002ever, the resulting triplets typically contain redun\u0002dant and ambiguous information, e.g., multiple\nL98@P1: semantically equivalent relation phrases such as\nL99@P1: ‘profession’, ‘job’, and ‘occupation’ (Kamp et al.,\nL100@P1: 2023; Putri et al., 2019; Vashishth et al., 2018).\nL101@P1: Phases 2 and 3 (Define and Canonicalize) stan\u0002dardize the triplets to make them useful for down\u0002stream tasks. We designed EDC to be flexible: it\nL102@P1: can either discover triplets consistent with a pre\u0002existing schema of potentially large size (Target\nL103@P1: Alignment) or self-generate a schema (Self Canon\u0002icalization). To achieve this, we use LLMs to de\u0002fine the schema components by exploiting their\nL104@P1: explanation generation capabilities — LLMs can\nL105@P1: justify their extractions via explanations that are\nL106@P1: agreeable to human experts (Li et al., 2023). The\nL107@P1: definitions are used to find the closest entity/rela\u0002tion type candidates (via a vector similarity search)\nL108@P1: that the LLM can then reference to canonicalize\nL109@P1: a component. In the case there is no equivalent\nL110@P1: counterpart in the existing schema, we can choose\nL111@P1: to add it to enrich the schema.\nL112@P1: To further improve performance, the three steps\nL113@P1: above can be followed by an additional Refine\u0002ment phase: we repeat EDC but provide the pre\u0002viously extracted triplets and a relevant part of the\nL114@P1: schema in the prompt during the initial extraction.\nL115@P1: We propose a trained Schema Retriever that re\u0002trieves schema components relevant to the input\nL116@P1: text, akin to retrieval-augmented generation (Lewis\nL117@P1: et al., 2020), which we find improves the generated\nL118@P1: triplets.\nL119@P1: Experiments on three KGC datasets in both Tar\u0002get Alignment and Self Canonicalization settings\nL120@P1: show that EDC is able to extract higher-quality\nL121@P1: KGs compared to state-of-the-art methods through\nL122@P1: both automatic and manual evaluation. Further\u0002more, the use of the Schema Retriever is shown\nL123@P1: to significantly and consistently improve EDC’s\nL124@P1: performance.\nL125@P1: In summary, the paper makes the following con\u0002tributions:\nL126@P1: • EDC, a flexible and performant LLM-based\nL127@P1: framework for knowledge graph construc\u0002tion that is able to extract high-quality KGs\nL128@P1: with schema of large size or without any pre\u0002defined schema.\nL129@P1: • Schema Retriever, a trained model to extract\nL130@P1: schema components relevant to input text in\nL131@P1: the same vein as information retrieval.\nL132@P1: • Empirical evidence that demonstrate the effec\u0002tiveness of EDC and the Schema Retriever.\nL133@P1-2: 98212 Background\nL134@P2: In this section, we provide relevant background on\nL135@P2: knowledge graph construction (KGC), open infor\u0002mation extraction (OIE), and canonicalization.\nL136@P2: Knowledge Graph Construction. Tradi\u0002tional methods typically addressed KGC using\nL137@P2: “pipelines”, comprising subtasks like entity\nL138@P2: discovery (Žukov-Gregoric et al. ˇ , 2018; Martins\nL139@P2: et al., 2019), entity typing (Choi et al., 2018; Onoe\nL140@P2: and Durrett, 2020), and relation classification\nL141@P2: (Zeng et al., 2014, 2015). Thanks to advances\nL142@P2: in pre-trained generative language models (e.g.,\nL143@P2: T5 (Raffel et al., 2020) and BERT(Lewis et al.,\nL144@P2: 2019)), more recent works instead frame KGC\nL145@P2: as a sequence-to-sequence problem and generate\nL146@P2: relational triplets in an end-to-end manner by\nL147@P2: fine-tuning these moderately-sized language\nL148@P2: models (Ye et al., 2022). The success of large\nL149@P2: language models (LLMs) has pushed this paradigm\nL150@P2: further: current methods directly prompt the\nL151@P2: LLMs to generate triplets in a zero/few-shot\nL152@P2: manner. For example, ChatIE (Wei et al., 2023)\nL153@P2: extracts triplets by framing the task as a multi-turn\nL154@P2: question-answering problem and CodeKGC (Bi\nL155@P2: et al., 2024) approaches the task as a code\nL156@P2: generation problem. As previously mentioned,\nL157@P2: these models face difficulties scaling up to general\nL158@P2: text common in many real-world applications as\nL159@P2: the KG schema has to be included in the LLM\nL160@P2: prompt. Our EDC framework circumvents this\nL161@P2: problem by using post-hoc canonicalization (and\nL162@P2: without requiring fine-tuning of the base LLMs).\nL163@P2: Open Information Extraction and Canonical\u0002ization. Standard (closed) information extraction\nL164@P2: requires the output triplets to follow a pre-defined\nL165@P2: schema, e.g. a list of relation or entity types to\nL166@P2: be extracted from. In contrast, open information\nL167@P2: extraction (OIE) does not have such a requirement.\nL168@P2: OIE has a long history and we refer readers who\nL169@P2: want comprehensive coverage to the excellent sur\u0002veys (Liu et al., 2022; Zhou et al., 2022; Kamp\nL170@P2: et al., 2023). Recent studies have found LLMs\nL171@P2: to exhibit excellent performance on OIE tasks (Li\nL172@P2: et al., 2023). However, the relational triplets ex\u0002tracted from OIE systems are not canonicalized;\nL173@P2: multiple semantically equivalent relations can co\u0002exist without being unified to a canonical form,\nL174@P2: causing redundancy and ambiguity in the induced\nL175@P2: open knowledge graph. An extra canonicalization\nL176@P2: step is required to standardize the triplets to make\nL177@P2: the KGs useful for downstream applications.\nL178@P2: Canonicalization methods differ depending on\nL179@P2: whether a target schema is available. In case a\nL180@P2: target schema is present, the task is sometimes re\u0002ferred to as “alignment” (Putri et al., 2019). For\nL181@P2: example, Putri et al. (2019) uses WordNet (Miller,\nL182@P2: 1995) as side information to obtain definitions for\nL183@P2: the OIE-extracted relation phrases and a Siamese\nL184@P2: network to compare an OIE relation definition and\nL185@P2: a pre-defined relation in the target schema. In case\nL186@P2: no target schema is available, state-of-the-art meth\u0002ods are commonly based on clustering (Vashishth\nL187@P2: et al., 2018; Dash et al., 2020). CESI (Vashishth\nL188@P2: et al., 2018) creates embeddings for the OIE rela\u0002tions using side information from external sources\nL189@P2: like PPDB (Ganitkevitch et al., 2013) and WordNet.\nL190@P2: However, clustering-based methods are prone to\nL191@P2: over-generalization (Kamp et al., 2023; Putri et al.,\nL192@P2: 2019), e.g., CESI may put “is brother of”, “is son\nL193@P2: of”, “is main villain of”, and “was professor of”\nL194@P2: into the same relation cluster.\nL195@P2: Compared to the existing canonicalization meth\u0002ods, EDC is more general; it works whether a\nL196@P2: target schema is provided or not. Instead of using\nL197@P2: static external sources like WordNet, EDC utilizes\nL198@P2: contextual and semantically-rich side information\nL199@P2: generated by LLMs. Furthermore, by allowing\nL200@P2: the LLMs to verify if a transformation can be per\u0002formed (instead of solely relying on the embedding\nL201@P2: similarity), EDC alleviates the over-generalization\nL202@P2: issue faced by previous methods.\nL203@P2: 3 Method: EDC for KGC\nL204@P2: This section outlines our primary contribution: an\nL205@P2: approach to constructing knowledge graphs that\nL206@P2: leverages LLMs in a structured manner. We first\nL207@P2: detail the EDC framework followed by a descrip\u0002tion of refinement (EDC+R). Given input text, our\nL208@P2: goal is to extract relational triplets in a canonical\nL209@P2: form such that the resulting KGs will have minimal\nL210@P2: ambiguity and redundancy. When there is a pre\u0002defined target schema, all generated triplets should\nL211@P2: conform to it. In the scenario where there is not\nL212@P2: one, the system should dynamically create one and\nL213@P2: canonicalize the triplets with respect to it.\nL214@P2: 3.1 EDC: Extract-Define-Canonicalize\nL215@P2: At a high level, EDC decomposes KGC into three\nL216@P2: connected subtasks. To ground our discussion, we\nL217@P2: will use a specific input text example: “Alan Shep\u0002ard was born on Nov 18, 1923 and selected by\nL218@P2-3: 9822NASA in 1959. He was a member of the Apollo 14\nL219@P3: crew” and walk through each of the phases:\nL220@P3: Phase 1: Open Information Extraction: we first\nL221@P3: leverage Large Language Models (LLMs) for open\nL222@P3: information extraction. Through few-shot prompt\u0002ing, LLMs identify and extract relational triplets\nL223@P3: ([Subject, Relation, Object]) from input texts, inde\u0002pendent of any specific schema. Using our example\nL224@P3: above, the prompt is:\nL225@P3: OIE Prompt\nL226@P3: Given a piece of text, extract relational triplets in\nL227@P3: the form of [Subject, Relation, Object] from it.\nL228@P3: Here are some examples:\nL229@P3: Example 1:\nL230@P3: Text: The 17068.8 millimeter long ALCO RS-3\nL231@P3: has a diesel-electric transmission.\nL232@P3: Triplets: [[‘ALCO RS-3’, ‘powerType’, ‘Diesel\u0002electric transmission’], [‘ALCO RS-3’, ‘length’,\nL233@P3: ‘17068.8 (millimetres)’]] ...\nL234@P3: Now please extract triplets from the following\nL235@P3: text: Alan Shepard was born on Nov 18, 1923\nL236@P3: and selected by NASA in 1959. He was a mem\u0002ber of the Apollo 14 crew.\nL237@P3: The resultant triplets (in this case, [‘Alan Shep\u0002ard’, ‘bornOn’, ‘Nov 18, 1923’], [‘Alan Shep\u0002ard’, ‘participatedIn’, ‘Apollo 14’]) form an open\nL238@P3: KG, which is forwarded to subsequent phases.\nL239@P3: Phase 2: Schema Definition: Next, we prompt\nL240@P3: the LLMs to provide a natural language definition\nL241@P3: for each component of the schema induced by the\nL242@P3: open KG:\nL243@P3: Schema Definition Prompt\nL244@P3: Given a piece of text and a list of relational triplets\nL245@P3: extracted from it, write a definition for each rela\u0002tion present.\nL246@P3: Example 1:\nL247@P3: Text: The 17068.8 millimeter long ALCO RS-3\nL248@P3: has a diesel-electric transmission.\nL249@P3: Triplets: [[‘ALCO RS-3’, ‘powerType’, ‘Diesel\u0002electric transmission’], [‘ALCO RS-3’, ‘length’,\nL250@P3: ‘17068.8 (millimetres)’]]\nL251@P3: Definitions:\nL252@P3: powerType: The subject entity uses the type of\nL253@P3: power or energy source specified by the object\nL254@P3: entity.\nL255@P3: ...\nL256@P3: Now write a definition for each relation present\nL257@P3: in the triplets extracted from the following text:\nL258@P3: Text: Alan Shepard was an American who was\nL259@P3: born on Nov 18, 1923 in New Hampshire, was\nL260@P3: selected by NASA in 1959, was a member of the\nL261@P3: Apollo 14 crew and died in California\nL262@P3: Triplets: [[‘Alan Shepard’, ‘bornOn’, ‘Nov 18,\nL263@P3: 1923’], [‘Alan Shepard’, ‘participatedIn’, ‘Apollo\nL264@P3: 14’]]\nL265@P3: This example prompt results in the definitions\nL266@P3: for (bornOn: The subject entity was born on\nL267@P3: the date specified by the object entity.) and\nL268@P3: (participatedIn: The subject entity took part in\nL269@P3: the event or mission specified by the object\nL270@P3: entity.), which are then passed to the next stage as\nL271@P3: side information used for canonicalization.\nL272@P3: Phase 3: Schema Canonicalization: The third\nL273@P3: phase aims to refine the open KG into a canoni\u0002cal form, eliminating redundancies and ambigui\u0002ties. We start by vectorizing the definitions of each\nL274@P3: schema component using a sentence transformer to\nL275@P3: create embeddings. Canonicalization then proceeds\nL276@P3: in one of two ways, depending on the availability\nL277@P3: of a target schema:\nL278@P3: • Target Alignment: With an existing target\nL279@P3: schema, we identify the most closely related\nL280@P3: components within the target schema for each\nL281@P3: element, considering them for canonicaliza\u0002tion. To prevent issues of over-generalization,\nL282@P3: LLMs assess the feasibility of each potential\nL283@P3: transformation. If a transformation is deemed\nL284@P3: unreasonable, indicating no semantic equiva\u0002lent in the target schema, the component, and\nL285@P3: its related triplets are excluded.\nL286@P3: • Self Canonicalization: Absent a target\nL287@P3: schema, the goal is to consolidate semanti\u0002cally similar schema components, standardiz\u0002ing them to a singular representation to stream\u0002line the KG. Starting with an empty canonical\nL288@P3: schema, we examine the open KG triplets,\nL289@P3: searching for potential consolidation candi\u0002dates through vector similarity and LLM veri\u0002fication. Unlike target alignment, components\nL290@P3: deemed non-transformable are added to the\nL291@P3: canonical schema, thereby expanding it.\nL292@P3: Using our example, the prompt is:\nL293@P3: Schema Canonicalization Prompt\nL294@P3: Given a piece of text, a relational triplet extracted\nL295@P3: from it, and the definition of the relation in it,\nL296@P3: choose the most appropriate relation to replace it\nL297@P3: in this context if there is any.\nL298@P3: Text: Alan Shepard was born on Nov 18, 1923\nL299@P3: and selected by NASA in 1959. He was a member\nL300@P3: of the Apollo 14 crew.\nL301@P3: Triplets: [‘Alan Shepard’, ‘participatedIn’,\nL302@P3: ‘Apollo 14’]\nL303@P3: Definition of ‘participatedIn’: The subject entity\nL304@P3-4: 9823took part in the event or mission specified by the\nL305@P4: object entity.\nL306@P4: Choices:\nL307@P4: A. ‘mission’: The subject entity participated in\nL308@P4: the event or operation specified by the object en\u0002tity.\nL309@P4: B. ‘season’: The subject entity participated in the\nL310@P4: season of a series specified by the object entity.\nL311@P4: ...\nL312@P4: F. None of the above\nL313@P4: Note that the choices above are obtained by us\u0002ing vector similarity search. After the LLM makes\nL314@P4: its choice, the relations are transformed to yield:\nL315@P4: [‘Alan Shepard’, ‘birthDate’, ‘Nov 18, 1923’],\nL316@P4: [‘Alan Shepard’, ‘mission’, ‘Apollo 14’], which\nL317@P4: forms our canonicalized KG.\nL318@P4: 3.2 EDC+R: iteratively refine EDC with\nL319@P4: Schema Retriever\nL320@P4: The refinement process leverages the data gener\u0002ated by EDC to enhance the quality of the extracted\nL321@P4: triplets. Inspired by retrieval-augmented genera\u0002tion and prior work (Bi et al., 2024), we construct\nL322@P4: a “hint” for the extraction phase (details in Ap\u0002pendix A.4), which comprises two main elements:\nL323@P4: • Candidate Entities: The entities extracted by\n"
